  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 329 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nAfter the upper-level trough finally accelerates and departs the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley, upper-level flow will become quasi-zonal \nevening Saturday into Sunday with a cold front reaching the CWA late \nSaturday and then stalling on Sunday. As a result, between 15 and 40 \npercent of ensemble model membership has showers and thunderstorms \nat times late Friday night through Sunday, especially across central \nand northeastern MO where large-scale forcing and nocturnal LLJs \nwill be strongest through the period. High temperatures will range \nfrom the low-80s F north of the front and where there are \nmorning/daytime showers and thunderstorms, to the low-90s F south of \nthe front where there should be less clouds.\n\nAn upper-level ridge will briefly dominate the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley late Sunday through Monday, helping the front lift northward \nout of the CWA which places more of the CWA firmly in the warm \nsector with 850-hPa temperatures increasing to the 95th \nclimatological percentile. These factors support Monday being the \nmost confident hot day of the period for most of the CWA with high \ntemperatures reaching the upper 80s to mid-90s F.\n\nGlobal models still vary in their depictions of the upper-level wave \npattern beyond Monday, but there is a growing signal for the upper-\nlevel ridge to shift across the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic, placing \nthe Mid-Mississippi River Valley in a period of deeper southwesterly \nflow for at least a couple days. What is still uncertain is how \nquickly the ridge breaks down and allows a cold front to gradually \nadvance southeastward into the region, breaking hot temperatures \nwith showers and thunderstorms and a much cooler airmass behind the \nfront. As a result, the NBM interquartile range of high temperatures \nincrease to around 10 F Tuesday, onward ranging from persistent \nseasonably warm or hot temperatures to temperatures closer to \nseasonal averages. It is also worth noting that part of this \nuncertain in the ridge break down is related to highly variable \nsolutions with tropical system development in the Atlantic Ocean \nmid to late next week.",
